00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:35.580
Theatre where Sarah and Mae used to work.
And if you pause the film right at this point, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:41.040
can also spot an advert for a crazy contraption&nbsp;
called an "Electropathic Belt" which promises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:46.380
to cure "weak and languid feelings", "nervousness&nbsp;
and rupture", "corpulence and general debility".
&nbsp;

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:51.720
Amazingly this isn't a made-up joke for the&nbsp;
movie, but a real device that was sold in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:57.900
Victorian London by the Medical Battery Company.
In fact, electric belts and other pseudo-medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:01.920
devices were widely promoted around&nbsp;
the time Enola Holmes is set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:06.600
and they were touted as being able to treat&nbsp;
a vast array of illnesses and conditions.
&nbsp;

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:11.820
A huge exposé was later run in the Pall&nbsp;
Mall Gazette, the newspaper that features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:16.860
in the movie, and which also had previously&nbsp;
run numerous ads for the very same belt.
